# Event Records Normalization Example

Here's a functional example that normalizes denormalized event records:

```javascript
// Denormalized event data
const rawEvents = [
  {
    id: 1,
    type: 'user_signup',
    user: { id: 101, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    timestamp: '2026-10-01T10:30:00Z',
    metadata: { source: 'web', ip: '192.168.1.1' }
  },
  {
    id: 2,
    type: 'user_login',
    user: { id: 101, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    timestamp: '2026-10-02T14:22:00Z',
    metadata: { source: 'mobile', ip: '192.168.1.2' }
  },
  {
    id: 3,
    type: 'purchase',
    user: { id: 102, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
    timestamp: '2026-10-03T09:15:00Z',
    metadata: { amount: 99.99, currency: 'USD' }
  }
];

// Normalize into separate entities
const normalize = (events) => ({
  users: extractUnique(events, 'user', 'id'),
  events: events.map(e => ({
    id: e.id,
    type: e.type,
    userId: e.user.id,
    timestamp: e.timestamp,
    metadata: e.metadata
  }))
});

const extractUnique = (data, path, keyField) =>
  Object.values(
    data.reduce((acc, item) => {
      const entity = item[path];
      return entity ? { ...acc, [entity[keyField]]: entity } : acc;
    }, {})
  );

// Usage
const normalized = normalize(rawEvents);
console.log(normalized);
```

**Output structure:**
```json
{
  "users": [
    { "id": 101, "name": "Alice", "email": "alice@example.com" },
    { "id": 102, "name": "Bob", "email": "bob@example.com" }
  ],
  "events": [
    { "id": 1, "type": "user_signup", "userId": 101, "timestamp": "...", "metadata": {...} },
    { "id": 2, "type": "user_login", "userId": 101, "timestamp": "...", "metadata": {...} },
    { "id": 3, "type": "purchase", "userId": 102, "timestamp": "...", "metadata": {...} }
  ]
}
```

**Benefits:**
- Eliminates data duplication (user info stored once)
- Easier to query and update
- Better performance for large datasets

# Normalizing Event Records with Functional Approach

Based on your preference for functional examples, here's a data transformation that normalizes event records:

```javascript
// Pure, composable functions for normalization

const normalizeDate = (dateStr) => 
  new Date(dateStr).toISOString();

const normalizeEventName = (name) => 
  name.trim().toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, '_');

const normalizeLocation = (loc) => ({
  latitude: Number(loc.lat || loc.latitude || 0),
  longitude: Number(loc.lng || loc.longitude || 0),
  address: String(loc.address || loc.place || '')
});

const normalizeEvent = (event) => ({
  id: event.id || event.event_id,
  name: normalizeEventName(event.name || event.title),
  timestamp: normalizeDate(event.date || event.created_at),
  location: normalizeLocation(event.location || {}),
  metadata: {
    source: event.source || 'unknown',
    tags: Array.isArray(event.tags) ? event.tags : []
  }
});

// Compose functions for pipeline processing
const pipe = (...fns) => (value) => fns.reduce((acc, fn) => fn(acc), value);

const processEvents = (events) => 
  events
    .map(normalizeEvent)
    .filter(e => e.id && e.timestamp)
    .sort((a, b) => new Date(a.timestamp) - new Date(b.timestamp));

// Example usage
const rawEvents = [
  { event_id: '1', title: 'Tech Meetup', date: '2026-10-15', lat: '40.7128', lng: '-74.0060', tags: ['tech', 'nyc'] },
  { id: '2', name: '  Annual Summit  ', created_at: '2026-10-20', location: { address: 'Boston' }, tags: [] }
];

const normalized = processEvents(rawEvents);
console.log(normalized);
```

**Key functional principles used:**
- **Pure functions** - each normalizer has no side effects
- **Composition** - small functions combined for larger transformations
- **Immutability** - original data unchanged
- **Higher-order functions** - `pipe` and `map/filter/sort` chains

